 you want  to bump up though some of those quality  settings as well so it is if you want  all that stuff you're gonna need to have  a fairly high end PC yeah I actually did  a couple of tests with one of our office  pcs which has a 20 80 TI and I also  tried to max out everything I turned  everything as high as I could  we turn it and I was getting yeah like  Max 80 minimum was like 40 and you could  definitely see it fluctuating at points  as I was playing but it is who a boy is  it a demanding game if you want to try  and turn on everything speaking of the  game being demanding I think one of the  most important aspects of this being a  PC version is framerate mmm-hmm now the  console versions were locked at about 30  frames per second which is fine it works  well for this style of game because this  is not a competitive FPS where a  framerate is going to be an inherent  advantage but it is nice to play this  game at a smooth 60 or higher framerate  I have a 144 Hertz monitor and boy  things look a lot smoother so I think  that's an aspect that a lot of PC gamers  are gonna want to get out of this now  the PC version looks incredible so does  the Xbox one X and ps4 Pro version so I  would say the PC version if you have  that high-end PC it's going to it can  look better than those console versions  but they all are visually stunning  regardless so here the leg up is having  that that added framerate and you know  it helps in combat we were talking about  how maybe it's a little bit easier to  aim now that you have a smoother frame  rate you're not fighting the game itself  in terms of trying to get headshots or  moving around the environment a little  bit more especially when the action gets  hot and there's a lot going on it's a  little bit easier to just kind of take  in the moment when you're in the middle  of a huge firefight when you have higher  frame rates so that that's a that's a  cool advantage to have that's inherent  to a PC version there are some visual  bugs though I noticed that trees  sometimes just get like glitchy and  start fizzing out in the like out in the  environment didn't happen that often it  wasn't game breaking or anything but it  was kind of noticeable when I'm was  chilling in the camp and I just see  trees freaking out and stuff yeah I  think it's cuz it looks so good most of  the time that is small stuff like that  that really isn't a big deal  right it's a little more noticeable  right a game like this I don't know if  this is specific to the PC version but I  did notice that when I go into first  person view and I'm talking to some of  the NPC's sometimes their mouths don't  move when they're saying something yeah  a little weird but again that is it is a  rare occasion and what it does happen  it's noticeable but other than that it's  there isn't much in terms of visual  books one thing I did notice that I'm  not sure how prevalent it is but I  noticed that there were some animations  that were actually a little lower frame  rate like I think of the strap on on  Arthur's gun was actually like the  animation on it seemed a little bit  aware then 60 frames a second which is  something that you never noticed on  console that's a little bit noticeable  on PC when you're running at higher  frames for the most part though like  that I just noticed it with that one  example I don't know how prevalent that  is but that's a minor thing that's worth  anything  one thing to note about the graphics  options though is that there are  multiple options for anti-aliasing which  is an important setting to use note that  msaa  is the most demanding so if you're gonna  crank that to either four times or eight  times to get the full effect you're  gonna take a big cut in frame rate so  what I've been doing is using temporal  anti-aliasing and then cracking up the  temporal sharpness to kind of get rid of  that softer look that it may give if you  don't crank up the schrödinger's now the  reason why the PC version and all its  graphics options are important is  because a lot of Red Dead Redemption too  has to do with your relationship to the  world when you're horseback riding to  from one town to another you're soaking  in the fastest of the open-world is how  how pretty it looks how the sunset looks  how the Sun blasts through the trees  when you're hunting and stuff like that  and a lot of it has to do with the  cinematic quality of its cutscenes and I  think that when you're playing on a ps4  pro or xbox 1 x version those qualities  shine through and it does is well on PC  so there's no huge advantage in terms of  you know is this a different experience  on PC when it comes to visuals like well  if you played on the upgraded consoles  other than framerate I think that you're  getting mostly the same type of  experience well then one of the things  that is potentially different on PC is  mounted keyboard support how did that  feel  keyboard mouse controls are important to  me even for games that aren't  competitive fps and I think they've  translated well to read that to some of  the menu stuff is weird because when you  pull up a menu and you want to look that  look at a diff  items my instinct is to hover over the  items with the mouse cursor to kind of  highlight it and see like it's details  and stuff the problem is that the game  doesn't recognize you hovering over them  as you know switching to I want to look  at what this item is  and instead you would have to use the  arrow keys to kind of shuffle through  your menu and but not WASD so that's  kind of your only option by default and  I think that that's a little weird  because I would rather have it if I  hover my mouse over it's like okay you  can highlight a different item and so  what I would do is click it and like  that would be to use it so that kind of  threw me off a little bit but these are  minor things in the grand scheme and you  know just being able to navigate  navigate the environment and use your  mouse for aiming is a really cool  feature to have of course and with the  Red Dead - there's so many different  systems and mechanics at play now you're  pulling out your satchel you go into  your horse looking into your inventory  they're picking up things looting stuff  and there's just so many different  controls that it's nice to have that on  a keyboard instead of maybe fumbling  through the controller for the first  couple of hours now when it comes to  controller support though that control  scheme has translated one-for-one from  the consoles so if you rather play with  the controller that control scheme is  fine just takes some getting used to and  you just plug in a dual shock for or an  Xbox one controller or even an Xbox 360  controller and you're good to go  straight off the bat well I think one  thing that's worth addressing even  though it might be resolved by the time  this video is out is the launch day  crashes that the game was having seemed  like it was having a bit of a rough  launch did you have any of that in your  experience I did I would crash to the  desktop when I would try to load the  story missions and I had one where it  was in the loading screen and it said it  exited the game exited unexpectedly then  another one where I was at least a  minute into the intro cutscene and then  it would crash there there were some  workarounds that I was able to figure  out such as using making sure that was  using DirectX 12 which is a menu option  also updating your drivers your video  drivers  I'm using AMD or Nvidia and make sure  that you're using the most up-to-date  drivers of course and a combination of  those things got me to run the game  smoothly later on on on launch day there  was a patch that rolled out and it seems  to have fixed most things for most  players  by all accounts so aside from a fairly  rough launch day I think that's you know  this game is up and running quite well  if you have the proper PC roxor did add  some content to the PC version mostly  side stuff that is largely  inconsequential to the main story but  they are there they have extra bounty  missions gang hideouts treasure maps as  a new stranger if you're looking out for  that some trinkets some weapons horses  but this is all complimentary stuff that  you know it's it's not going to affect  the overall experience and but those  those things are there there's also a  photo mode so if you like taking  pictures gonna get Arthur looking all  nice and stuff and or as dirty as you  want really because decimal can get real  dirty and take some nice photos you have  some options like contrast filters  changing camera angles themselves  because they have a couple that make it  look like the photos and the loading  screen and stuff yeah and you know Red  Dead Redemption who has such wonderfully  detailed environments that in that game  is prime for a photo mode that about  covers everything I want to talk about  in terms of the Red Dead Redemption to  PC version so if you haven't played this  game it's it's an absolutely wonderful  game we gave it our game to be here for  2018 so has our stamp of approval and  yeah if you have if you want to play  this on PC if you've been holding out  this is definitely you know a version  that you can play that's Oren's properly  at least now it does alright well thank  you very much Michael and for any other  Red Dead related coverage or just any  coverage about any video game really  stay tuned to game spot  [Music]  you  [Music]

